# Rank 1228 / CVE-2026-5616 (GHSA-52xr-h9vp-pxcj) jeecgboot/JeecgBoot: the AI
# chat module hands the language model a LangChain4j tool registry that includes
# account administration. JeecgBizToolsProvider.getDefaultTools() built four
# ToolSpecification/ToolExecutor pairs -- query_user_by_name, add_user,
# query_all_roles and grant_user_roles -- and returned all four unconditionally,
# and AiragChatServiceImpl.sendWithDefault() attached that map to every
# conversation running the default app. Which tool runs is decided by the model
# from the prompt the caller wrote, so any caller of the chat endpoint could ask
# the assistant to create an account and grant it roles (CWE-862, CWE-306).
#
# Pool commit 8c3410defc45971ee954e684b6d412c1e80a7da7 is the pull request's
# merge commit rather than an authored fix, which is why the advisory metadata
# reports a commit mismatch: the advisory names the two commits it merges,
# b7c9aeba7aefda9e008ea8fe4fc3daf08d0c5b39 and
# 2c1cc88b8d983868df8c520a343d6ff4369d9e59. The merge's combined diff is exactly
# those two and nothing else, and its first parent 05b9b5e4 is the vulnerable
# state, so it works as a fix commit. The durable row is the branch head
# 2c1cc88b, which carries the JeecgBizToolsProvider.java change the advisory
# names as the affected component. b7c9aeba is the other half: it makes
# AiragChatServiceImpl.sendWithDefault() attach the default tools only when the
# request carries a verifiable token.
#
# Repository names: rank 1227 scans jeecgboot/jeecg-boot and this rank scans
# jeecgboot/JeecgBoot. Both clones share the root commit
# e7fa2b23413a7320fd51ae0c33d16c43637320d0 and the same history, so these are one
# repository under an old and a current name, not two projects.
#
# The snippets below model the registry half, the one the advisory names.
# 2c1cc88b wraps each tools.put in SecurityUtils.getSubject().isPermitted(...),
# so a caller only ever receives the tools whose permission it already holds.
# The project's main branch carries a reworked form of the same idea, and the
# third test models it: currentUserPermissions() resolves the request's token
# into a permission set through sysUserService.getUserPermissionsSet, returns an
# empty registry when that set is empty, and tests membership per tool. Both
# forms have to be rejected, because the second one is what the project actually
# ships.
#
# FIXED. Nothing labelled the registry: a bounded scan of both real revisions of
# JeecgBizToolsProvider.java reports no finding on either side without the
# binding below.
# Added code.LlmToolRegistrationMissingAuthorization (VYQL-AUTH-112) and a Java
# binding at class scope: the class names both LangChain4j registry types
# (ToolSpecification and ToolExecutor), calls an account, role or permission
# administration operation, and contains no authorization check. Two kinds of
# check suppress it, because applications use both: a framework call that asks
# the current subject directly (isPermitted, checkPermission, hasAuthority,
# hasRole, isAuthenticated, getAuthentication and the rest) or a method
# annotation that declares the requirement (RequiresPermissions, PreAuthorize,
# Secured, RolesAllowed and the rest), and a permission-named lookup that loads
# the caller's permission set once so each tool can test membership against it.
#
# All three conjuncts are needed. A registry alone is not a finding: the fourth
# test below is a LangChain4j provider whose only tool reads the weather, the
# shape LangChain4j's own documentation and examples use, and it is rejected
# because no administration operation appears. An administration call alone is
# not a finding either: the fifth test is an ordinary user-management service
# that creates accounts and grants roles, and it is rejected because nothing in
# it names the registry types.
#
# Measured on the real checkout, bounded (timeout 180, --max-ram 4GB,
# --cache off). Scanning jeecg-boot/jeecg-module-system at the vulnerable parent
# 05b9b5e4 (383 java files) reports 276 findings, one of them VYQL-AUTH-112 at
# JeecgBizToolsProvider.java:35; scanning the same module at the merge commit
# reports 275, the same set minus that finding. The whole repository exceeds the
# 180s bound, so the module holding the changed file is the real-checkout
# evidence. False positives: the same binding over two unrelated Java corpora
# already cloned for this corpus, james-project/server (3231 java files) and
# jackson-databind/src (721 java files), reports VYQL-AUTH-112 zero times.

test "rank1228: LangChain4j tool registry exposes account administration with no permission check"
  lang java
  file JeecgBizToolsProvider.java
  expect VYQL-AUTH-112
  expect_review code.LlmToolRegistrationMissingAuthorization
  code
  ```
  package org.jeecg.modules.airag;

  import dev.langchain4j.agent.tool.ToolSpecification;
  import dev.langchain4j.model.chat.request.json.JsonObjectSchema;
  import dev.langchain4j.service.tool.ToolExecutor;

  public class JeecgBizToolsProvider implements JeecgToolsProvider {

      @Autowired
      private ISysUserService sysUserService;

      public Map<ToolSpecification, ToolExecutor> getDefaultTools() {
          Map<ToolSpecification, ToolExecutor> tools = new HashMap<>();
          JeecgLlmTools addUser = addUserTool();
          tools.put(addUser.getToolSpecification(), addUser.getToolExecutor());
          return tools;
      }

      private JeecgLlmTools addUserTool() {
          ToolSpecification toolSpecification = ToolSpecification.builder()
                  .name("add_user")
                  .description("add a user and return the result")
                  .parameters(
                          JsonObjectSchema.builder()
                                  .addStringProperty("username", "user name")
                                  .addStringProperty("password", "password")
                                  .required("username", "password")
                                  .build()
                  )
                  .build();
          ToolExecutor toolExecutor = (toolExecutionRequest, memoryId) -> {
              JSONObject arguments = JSONObject.parseObject(toolExecutionRequest.arguments());
              SysUser user = JSON.parseObject(arguments.toJSONString(), SysUser.class);
              String salt = oConvertUtils.randomGen(8);
              user.setSalt(salt);
              user.setPassword(PasswordUtil.encrypt(user.getUsername(), user.getPassword(), salt));
              sysUserService.saveUser(user, null, null, null, false);
              return "user added";
          };
          return new JeecgLlmTools(toolSpecification, toolExecutor);
      }
  }
  ```

test "rank1228: each tool is registered only when the caller holds its permission"
